n be messy.** The "dark side" error is a common semantic trap. Analogies in school (like the spinning room) are meant to help, but they can be simplified to the point of inaccuracy. The danger is when the catchy phrase replaces the actual physics. This is where errors creep in. 3. **You're learning to be skeptical, not cynical.** The goal isn't to believe nothing; it's to ask "How do we know this?" If you'd asked your teacher that back then, it might have sparked a useful discussion. For anything you're unsure of, you can look for the evidence that supports it. So, what should you do with your old school knowledge? Don't throw it all out. Use this experience as a turning point. You've just upgraded from a student who absorbs facts to a person who understands that knowledge is earned by a process that sometimes makes mistakes. The same scientific method that corrected this lunar misconception is the exact method that can be trusted to correct other errors, too. The system works precisely because people are willing to say, "Wait, that can't be right, let's check." You're now one of those people. That's a good thing.
